{{define "content"}}
<vox-hero eyebrow="Orchestration" heading="Run it now, everywhere" data-i18n-attr="heading,eyebrow">
  <p data-i18n>
    Trigger Puppet runs, tasks and plans against the nodes you pick, and
    watch the results come back one node at a time.
  </p>
</vox-hero>

<section class="site-section">
  <h2 class="vox-ts-xxl" data-i18n>Results per node, not per job</h2>
  <div class="vox-prose">
    <p data-i18n>
      Every target is recorded on its own: status, exit code and output.
      A job that worked on nine nodes and failed on one says exactly that,
      instead of collapsing to a single verdict you then have to go and
      investigate.
    </p>
    <p data-i18n>
      Where a job caused a Puppet run, its result links straight to the
      report that run produced &mdash; so "what did this actually change"
      is one click away.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{{screenshot "jobs-list"}}

<vox-timeline>
  <vox-timeline-item heading="Job created" data-i18n-attr="heading">Targets recorded, each one pending.</vox-timeline-item>
  <vox-timeline-item heading="Dispatched" data-i18n-attr="heading">Work goes out to the nodes.</vox-timeline-item>
  <vox-timeline-item heading="Results arrive" data-i18n-attr="heading">Each node reports back as it finishes.</vox-timeline-item>
  <vox-timeline-item heading="Job completed" data-i18n-attr="heading">The job takes the status its targets add up to.</vox-timeline-item>
</vox-timeline>

<vox-callout variant="tip" heading="Built to keep up" data-i18n-attr="heading">
  <p data-i18n>
    Orchestration runs over NATS, so reaching a large fleet is a matter of
    adding capacity rather than reworking how messages are routed.
  </p>
</vox-callout>
{{end}}
